      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Characteristics of Lithospheric Plates"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Lithospheric plates are rigid slabs of solid rock that move horizontally over the semi-fluid asthenosphere.",
        "The Earth's surface is divided into seven major plates and numerous minor plates.",
        "Oceanic plates are predominantly basaltic, thinner, and denser, whereas continental plates are granitic, thicker, and less dense.",
        "Tectonic activity such as earthquakes, mountain building, and volcanic eruptions occur predominantly along plate boundaries."
      ],
      "id": "x5y5e0a"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "A lithospheric plate is a massive, irregularly-shaped slab of solid rock, generally composed of both continental and oceanic lithosphere.",
      "id": "dlf5yap"
    },
